---
description: Use this rule when generating or updating API routers. It enforces a consistent response format ({"data": [...]}), prevents redundant foreign keys in request bodies, and promotes clean multi-tenant patterns. Always validate parent scope (e.g., resource) via the URL, not the payload.
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---
In the routers directory:

* Create a file named <model>_router.py
* Define an APIRouter instance with:
* prefix="/<model_plural>" or for nested: prefix="/resources/{resource_id}/<model_plural>"
* tags=["<Model>"]
* Implement the following endpoints:
* GET / – list all items, returns { "data": [...] }
* GET /{id} – retrieve a specific item by ID
* POST / – create a new item
* PUT /{id} – update an existing item
* DELETE /{id} – delete an item
* Do not use underscore for path definition. A model named "RealEstate" should have a path "real-states"
* Use the corresponding service (<Model>Service) for database interaction
* Use Pydantic schemas for validation (<Model>CreateRequest, <Model>Update)
* Do not include resource_id in the request body schema if it’s passed via the URL
* Use routers/utils/dependencies when possible

Nested Resources

For resource-scoped routes:
* Use prefix="/resource/{[resource]_id}/<resource_plural>"
* Accept resource_id as a path parameter in each route function
* Always call get_resource_by_id(resource, db) at the start of the route to:
* Validate the resource exists
* Enforce access control and scope
* Inject resource_id manually into service-layer calls, not the request body

```python
@router.post("/", response_model=CredentialRead)
def create_credential(
    resource_id: UUID,
    data: CredentialCreateRequest,
    db: Session = Depends(get_db),
):
    resource = get_resource_by_id(resource_id, db)
    return CredentialService(db).create_credential(data, resource_id=resource.id)
```
